it was plenty of warning before typhoon  hagupit arrived but the power of the  storm caught some of Japan's most  vulnerable by surprise intense rainfall  cut off many people and communities  including dozens of elderly in a  retirement home in calico air northwest  of the capital Tokyo after the storm  passed they were brought to safety by  rescue workers the storm brought  damaging winds in excess of 200  kilometers an hour but the main feature  was a huge amount of rainfall some areas  saw a meter in 24 hours and waterways  simply couldn't cope the embankments of  more than 20 rivers collapsed one of the  worst hit areas was Nagano Prefecture  west of Tokyo where a 70 meter stretch  of the levee gave way many homes and  businesses were flooded and Japan's  famous bullet trains were surrounded by  water and going nowhere fast or slow ma  tante who the major typhoon has caused  extensive damage far and wide in eastern  Japan at one point the special warning  for heavy rain was issued for as many as  13 prefectures there has also been  emergency release from various dams and  flooding of various rivers it turned out  to be a record heavy rain more than a  hundred thousand rescue workers were  deployed including 31,000 from the  self-defense forces millions were  ordered or advised to evacuate but some  chose not to leave their homes or  couldn't get out in time Japanese are  used to preparing for storms and other  natural disasters but this one was  different it may have come and gone  quickly but many here say it was the  largest most intense typhoon they've  experienced and they're wondering  because of climate change if it's a sign  of things to come  in some places a recovery has begun but  this was a storm that claimed many lives  and caused vast amounts of damage the  full extent of which won't be known for  some time with so many areas still  inundated Wayne hey al Jazeera Tokyo
 

